#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da18d54e-338c-5e48-b6fc-70d88aab8558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},2,-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345944} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbee033-e156-5ff3-95a3-138ca4d5c4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345945} {:answer/id #uuid "f5dbc097-50db-5513-9857-bbeb935e5a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345946} {:answer/id #uuid "ada8711a-30a7-5ce1-a881-617f7ec61aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345947} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb9aee8-8356-528e-80c6-ded7e7e6edb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfbee033-e156-5ff3-95a3-138ca4d5c4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345945} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3efd32-0622-5e33-9ee0-6f14c847ba4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d442f185-bfd4-5e42-90bb-3802ec3f9725" :question/math-academy-id "q-127219" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,\\,2(1),\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c4fac2-5cd0-59da-b5dd-c3868164684f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,i-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345951} {:answer/id #uuid "e464aa71-44eb-5651-9949-61916d9f5ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{1}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345952} {:answer/id #uuid "4abc8301-23ba-5eed-9eb0-c32f04b6b7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345953} {:answer/id #uuid "2261493a-c67f-514a-a368-445207946f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345954} {:answer/id #uuid "66cf7ead-80cf-503e-82db-b5bfb0710f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66cf7ead-80cf-503e-82db-b5bfb0710f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345955} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b99139d-03ba-5e91-a86f-0ac1642ef68c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a941148-69a9-5b56-a529-c065abef2a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-127274" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, given that  $p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{3}\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2(i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k)+(1)(\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)+\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9+12} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbe6e2c-9f38-5046-8118-1709b12670ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345958} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca83f84-be9c-569a-ab34-c6316947c9cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345959} {:answer/id #uuid "d4806492-86a6-508a-b497-84e880380c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345960} {:answer/id #uuid "d39c615f-4187-51f3-906b-e24cbd71b112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345961} {:answer/id #uuid "cd26cde4-5780-5280-9991-82a361d6ecea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbe6e2c-9f38-5046-8118-1709b12670ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345958} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebbe877f-f60c-5587-bcdd-8889e9b0e50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "927bdcac-2749-5a1c-8331-70b709fa149b" :question/math-academy-id "q-33132" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨-{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0),\\,-{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{e}^{-(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0),\\,-{e}^{-(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-0+1,\\,-1-0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf492066-9e51-5541-9868-96c1b6e7118c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345965} {:answer/id #uuid "13dfad98-bcb3-515c-8a02-c2eab6678ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345966} {:answer/id #uuid "b7265abd-f54e-5de7-bdaf-a1190cafe99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345967} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b97864-9abf-543b-9185-0e23902868d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345968} {:answer/id #uuid "9adfeb6f-5280-50b3-9619-76e04619052d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b97864-9abf-543b-9185-0e23902868d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345968} :answer-field/id #uuid "53931b32-a748-562a-87ef-8728de14a6f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a50072c1-31e8-5068-bc13-cf7f64e8653d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33676" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve   $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,2{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩$  at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,-4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b172407d-6fb5-527e-8190-150ebcc8ef52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345972} {:answer/id #uuid "95bd15d9-145c-562d-abfd-78955c4c9e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(i+2j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345973} {:answer/id #uuid "4b55eb8e-4621-5ac5-b26b-fee7c0418161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345974} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9b98d2-8024-50a5-837b-90c05ce601d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(-2i+3j-2k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345975} {:answer/id #uuid "574617e0-a18f-5ce8-8d89-13c4c95c4b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(-2i+j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b55eb8e-4621-5ac5-b26b-fee7c0418161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345974} :answer-field/id #uuid "774b9b67-a04e-5b4a-89f1-df4d6b0ab1e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02973693-4a82-55bc-89aa-9c025052790c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75148" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}+2)p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=-1$, given that  $p(-1)=i+2\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(-1)=i+\\,j-\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}+2)p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\,p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)\\,p(-1)+((-1)^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\,p(-1)+3\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2(i+2\\,j-k)+3(i+\\,j-\\,k) \\\\  & =-2i-4\\,j+2k+3i+3j-3\\,k \\\\  & =i-j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(-1)}{‖{r}^{′}(-1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (i-j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ is  $T(-1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k).$"}]}
